I wrote `tests/public/test_uniform_sum.py`. `compile_check.py` reports no errors, and all 10 tests fail with a Runtime Error, as expected while the program still raises.

`spec.md` is missing three things the brief asked me to cover, so I couldn't write some of the requested tests:

- **No samples.** The statement has none, so there are no sample tests.
- **No constraints.** It gives no limits on N or on element values, so I couldn't test the largest allowed value. I kept every case small (N ≤ 4, values ≤ 10). The smallest-input test assumes N = 1 is allowed, which the spec doesn't say.
- **No format for blanks.** The spec says entries can be blank but not how a blank is written in the input. This looks like AtCoder ARC195 B, where a blank is `-1`, but the spec never says so. Since tests may only assert what the spec states, no test contains a blank. That means the main part of the problem, filling in blanks, has no test coverage.

What the tests do cover is the no-blank version: can A be reordered so every A_i + B_i is the same? The cases include:
- N = 1, which is always Yes;
- all zeros;
- input that is already uniform;
- input that becomes uniform only after reordering;
- two- and three-element Yes and No cases;
- a No case where the total of all values (13) can't be split into 3 equal sums.

I worked out each expected answer by hand and noted the reasoning in a comment on the case.

If you add the blank format and the constraints to `spec.md`, I can add tests for blanks and for the boundary values.

10 tests, in 1 file.
